add(1613,'nyquist-policy','Sampling rate versus policy rate','Why does a 100 Hz RL update rate not alone prove that the IMU “must run at least 200 Hz”?',[
('Sampling requirements depend on sensed-signal bandwidth, filtering, latency and the estimation/control design, not just twice the policy rate.','Nyquist concerns signal frequency content.'),('Every IMU must instead run at exactly 100 Hz, regardless of dynamics.','That is another unsupported universal rate rule.'),('A slower IMU always contains more high-frequency information because it reports fewer samples.','Lower sampling does not create bandwidth.'),('A faster IMU makes timestamp alignment and anti-alias filtering unnecessary.','Rate alone does not remove those requirements.')],'A two-times-policy-rate design choice may be useful in a particular system, but is not a direct universal sampling theorem.',kind='table',conflict='The sampling-rate row asserts a required IMU rate from only the policy step frequency, without signal-bandwidth or system assumptions.',locator='Sampling rate row: RL 100 Hz → IMU at least 200 Hz.')
